.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SCHED_SETSCHEDULER 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sched_setscheduler ,
.Nm sched_getscheduler ,
.Nm sched_setparam ,
.Nm sched_getparam ,
.Nm sched_get_priority_max ,
.Nm sched_get_priority_min ,
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval ,
.Nm sched_setaffinity ,
.Nm sched_getaffinity ,
.Nm sched_yield ,
.Nm getcpu
.Nd scheduling policy, priority, affinity and yielding
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sched.h
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setscheduler "pid_t pid" "int policy" "const struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getscheduler "pid_t pid"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setparam "pid_t pid" "const struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getparam "pid_t pid" "struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_get_priority_max "int policy"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_get_priority_min "int policy"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval "pid_t pid" "struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setaffinity "pid_t pid" "size_t cpusetsize" "const cpu_set_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getaffinity "pid_t pid" "size_t cpusetsize" "cpu_set_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_yield "void"
.Ft int
.Fn getcpu "unsigned int *cpu" "unsigned int *node"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The scheduler runs real-time threads before ordinary threads, using the
highest base or inherited priority.
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
threads keep the processor against equal-priority peers until a yield or
block; equal-priority
.Dv SCHED_RR
threads rotate each tick.
A PI futex waiter donates its effective priority through its blocked owners
until unlock, timeout or interruption removes the donation.
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
and
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
have priority 0 and rotate in the ordinary ready order.
Each processor has its own run queue: a woken process joins the
processor it last ran on unless that one is busy and another it may run
on is idle, and a processor with nothing to run takes a process from the
busiest queue.
A
.Fa pid
of 0 names the caller; any other names a process that has not been
reaped.
.Pp
.Fn sched_getscheduler
returns the thread's policy,
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
.Pq 0 ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
.Pq 3
or
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
.Pq 5 ,
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
.Pq 1
or
.Dv SCHED_RR
.Pq 2 ,
with
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
added when it was set with it, and
.Fn sched_getparam
stores its base priority, without a PI donation.
.Fn sched_setscheduler
sets any of the three with priority 0, with
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
or without, for a caller that may change the thread: one with effective
user ID 0, or whose effective user ID equals the thread's real or
effective user ID.
.Fn sched_setparam
keeps the policy and changes its base priority.
Without privilege a caller may not leave
.Dv SCHED_IDLE ,
which Linux counts as nice 20 and lets a thread leave only as far as
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE ,
0 here, allows, and may not clear
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK ;
either fails with
.Er EPERM .
A fork's child has its parent's policy without
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK ,
which Linux clears in the child and which resets only the real-time and
deadline policies, and an exec keeps both.
A privileged caller can set
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
or
.Dv SCHED_RR
with priority 1 to 99.
An unprivileged caller may keep its existing real-time policy and lower
its priority, but cannot raise it or enter a real-time policy.
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
returns a real-time child to
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
at priority 0.
Other policies are refused with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
and
.Fn sched_get_priority_min
give Linux's range for each policy Linux has, whether or not it runs here:
1 to 99 for
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR ,
and 0 to 0 for
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH ,
.Dv SCHED_IDLE ,
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
and
.Dv SCHED_EXT .
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval
stores the round-robin time slice, one 1 ms tick.
.Pp
.Fn sched_getaffinity
stores the processors the process may run on, of those started, a bit
for each, and returns the bytes it stored: a long for every 32 or 64
processors up to the highest started, as Linux sizes its mask.
.Fn sched_setaffinity
reads at most that many bytes of the mask, binds the process to the
started processors the mask has, whatever it says of others, and moves a
process that runs on a processor it no longer has: the caller before the
call returns, another process at its processor's next tick.
A fork passes the set on, and an exec keeps it.
.Pp
.Fn getcpu
stores the number of the processor the caller runs on, which may change as
soon as the call returns, and 0 for the NUMA node, where its non-null
arguments point.
On x86-64, musl and glibc call it through the vDSO, which reads the number
with
.Ic rdtscp
and enters the kernel only on a processor without that instruction
.Pq Xr vdso 7 .
.Pp
.Fn sched_yield
puts the caller behind the other ready processes in the round-robin
order.
It returns when the caller's turn comes again, at once when no other
process is ready.
.Ss Differences from Linux
Linux grants
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR
to a caller with
.Dv CAP_SYS_NICE
or a
.Dv RLIMIT_RTPRIO
that allows the priority.
This kernel refuses them, to root too.
Linux does not let a
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
thread that wakes take the processor from the running one, and gives a
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
thread a weight far below nice 19's; here both take their turn as
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
does.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 154 for
.Fn sched_setparam ,
155 for
.Fn sched_getparam ,
156 for
.Fn sched_setscheduler ,
157 for
.Fn sched_getscheduler ,
158 for
.Fn sched_yield ,
159 for
.Fn sched_get_priority_max ,
160 for
.Fn sched_get_priority_min ,
161 for
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval ,
241 for
.Fn sched_setaffinity ,
and 242 for
.Fn sched_getaffinity ;
on x86-64 they are 142 to 148 in the same order but for
.Fn sched_yield ,
which is 24, and 203 and 204.
The raw
.Fn sched_getaffinity
returns the bytes of the mask it wrote, a long for each long's bits of
processors up to the highest started: four on i386 with 32 processors or
fewer, and eight with more and on x86-64.
It fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a
.Fa cpusetsize
shorter than that or not a multiple of a long; the C library clears the
rest of a larger set and returns 0.
The raw
.Fn sched_setaffinity
reads at most that many bytes of the mask.
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval
writes a 32-bit timespec.
An i386 program on the x86-64 kernel, which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, has the i386 calls: its mask is of 32-bit longs, four bytes
with 32 processors or fewer, as on i386,
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval
writes a 32-bit timespec, and
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval_time64
(423) a timespec of 64-bit fields.
.Pp
musl implements
.Fn sched_setscheduler ,
.Fn sched_getscheduler ,
.Fn sched_setparam
and
.Fn sched_getparam
as failures with
.Er ENOSYS
and does not call the kernel, since Linux's calls act on a thread and
POSIX's on a process; glibc calls it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sched_getscheduler
returns the policy,
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
and
.Fn sched_get_priority_min
the priority, and the other functions 0.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/schedmusl.c
yields, reads the policy, the parameter, the priority ranges, the time
slice and the affinity, which is every processor
.Pa /proc/cpuinfo
lists, sets
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
and
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
and reads each back, sets an affinity of the last processor alone, after which
.Fn getcpu
returns that processor, and checks the refusals of the other policies, of
an affinity of a processor past the last, and of another user's process.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A parameter, time slice or mask cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A
.Fa pid
is negative, a
.Fa param
is null, a policy or priority is one this kernel does not run, a policy
number is unknown to
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
or
.Fn sched_get_priority_min ,
a
.Fa cpusetsize
for
.Fn sched_getaffinity
is shorter than the mask or not a multiple of a long, or a mask for
.Fn sched_setaffinity
has no processor that is started.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not change the process, asked for
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
or
.Dv SCHED_RR ,
or, without privilege, asked to leave
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
or to clear
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK .
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process has the ID
.Fa pid .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getpriority 2 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr vdso 7
